{#- A radargram's own edits, shared by the catalog's card menus and the
    viewer's Edit menu (#431). Driven by radargram_edit.js, which finds its
    buttons by `data-edit-properties` and `data-replace-radargram`. The
    including page renders it only for someone who may edit the project. -#}
{#- One dialog for every card, filled in from the API when it opens: the
    values it shows include what each field would be *without* its
    override, which the card does not know and which is what makes
    "revert to the file" honest rather than a leap of faith. -#}
<dialog id="properties-dialog">
  <h2 id="properties-title">Edit properties</h2>
  <p class="hint">
    These are the project's own labels. They sit over what the radargram
    file says and never change the file, so reprocessing it does not lose
    them — and reverting a field puts the file's own value back.
  </p>
  <div id="properties-error" class="warning" hidden></div>
  <div class="properties-fields">
    <label>Name
      <input type="text" id="properties-display-name" autocomplete="off">
    </label>
    <p class="hint" id="properties-display-name-file"></p>

    {#- Membership, and only membership. Picking an existing group joins
        it; renaming one is the group's own dialog, reached from its
        heading. The first version of this was a text box with a datalist,
        where typing over a chosen name silently forked a new group
        instead of renaming the old one -- two operations that looked
        identical and were not. -#}
    <label>Group
      <select id="properties-group-choice">
        <option value="inherit">Use the radargram's own</option>
        <option value="ungrouped">No group</option>
        <option value="new">New group&hellip;</option>
      </select>
    </label>
    <label id="properties-group-row">New group's name
      <input type="text" id="properties-group-name" autocomplete="off">
    </label>
    <p class="hint" id="properties-group-file"></p>

    <label class="checkbox">
      <input type="checkbox" id="properties-unlisted">
      Unlisted
    </label>
    <p class="hint">
      Leaves this radargram out of listings and out of merged downloads.
      It is not private: anyone who knows the address can still open it.
    </p>

    {#- Bounds for the viewer's topographic correction. A property of the
        survey's GPS track, not of whoever is looking at it, so it is
        edited here rather than in the viewer -- which points back at
        this dialog when a radargram's elevations look wrong. -#}
    <fieldset class="field-group">
      <legend>Topographic correction (m)</legend>
      <div class="field-row">
        <label>Floor
          <input type="number" id="properties-elevation-min" step="any" autocomplete="off">
        </label>
        <label>Surface cap
          <input type="number" id="properties-elevation-max" step="any" autocomplete="off">
        </label>
      </div>
    </fieldset>
    <p class="hint">
      Bounds the corrected view in the viewer. <strong>Floor</strong> is the
      lowest elevation it draws — anything below is cropped.
      <strong>Surface cap</strong> is the highest a trace's surface may be;
      higher ones are pulled down to it, so a GPS spike cannot stretch the
      view. Leave blank for no bound.
    </p>

    {#- #411. How this line reads best, so it outranks a person's own
        default; the viewer's dropdown still changes it for one visit. -#}
    <label>Horizontal scale
      <select id="properties-x-scale">
        <option value="">Not set (uses your setting or the project&rsquo;s)</option>
        {% for scale in x_scales %}
        <option value="{{ scale.text }}">{{ scale.label }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
    </label>
    <p class="hint">
      What the viewer opens this radargram at, before anyone&rsquo;s own
      setting or the project default.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <button type="button" id="properties-save">Save</button>
    <button type="button" id="properties-close">Cancel</button>
  </p>
</dialog>

{#- What replacing this radargram would do, before it does any of it.
    The upload happens first -- what it costs cannot be known until the
    new file has been read -- and this is where the operator decides
    whether to keep it. Closing without confirming discards it. -#}
<dialog id="replace-dialog">
  <h2 id="replace-title">Replace radargram</h2>
  <p id="replace-status" class="hint"></p>
  {#- An explicit control rather than only the automatic one that opens
      with the dialog. Cancelling the operating system's file chooser
      fires no `change` event, so without this the dialog sat there with
      nothing chosen and no way back to the chooser except closing and
      reopening it. -#}
  <p>
    <button type="button" id="replace-choose">Choose file&hellip;</button>
  </p>
  <p id="replace-headline"></p>
  <div id="replace-detail"></div>
  <div id="replace-error" class="warning" hidden></div>
  <p>
    <button type="button" id="replace-confirm" class="danger" disabled>Replace</button>
    <button type="button" id="replace-close">Cancel</button>
  </p>
</dialog>

<input type="file" id="replace-radargram-file" accept=".nc" hidden>
